ZXPACK(1)                                           ZX-UX

NAME
    zxpack - save RAM by packing files

USE
    zxpack
    zxpack file ...
    zxpack -d file ...

WHAT IT DOES
    Files share a memory area with running programs.
    A file can be stored in full or in packed form.
    Reading or writing a packed file restores it first.
    Programs do not need to know how the file is stored.

    The system may pack closed files when no program is running.
    It first looks for files idle for 15 seconds.
    It stops packing when a process needs to run.
    If memory becomes full, it packs closed files and tries again.

    With no names, zxpack shows stored files and free RAM.
    With names, it packs those files at once.
    Use -d to restore the full form of named files.

    ls -l shows z for a packed file and its original size.
    du shows the memory that the stored form takes.

FORMAT
    The method uses LZ77 with a 2 KB window.
    An adaptive binary range coder stores matches and literals.
    Packing takes time and needs room for the new stream.
    Restoring a file needs room for its full size.

MESSAGES
    open, not packed     Another program is using the file.
    would not be smaller Packing would not save space.
    no room             Not enough free RAM.
